WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.900
- [Narrator] Let's say you wanted

00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:02.970
to buy this Primark T-shirt.

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.860
If you're shopping on Primark's website,

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.193
you'll quickly discover that you can't.

00:00:07.193 --> 00:00:08.280
(buzzer dings)

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.430
Over the past 10 years,

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:11.940
e-commerce sales have more than doubled.

00:00:11.940 --> 00:00:13.050
But last year,

00:00:13.050 --> 00:00:16.718
European fast fashion retailer
Primark made 9.5 billion

00:00:16.718 --> 00:00:19.140
in sales revenue without a single penny

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:20.670
from online shopping.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:21.503
- We made a very clear choice

00:00:21.503 --> 00:00:24.660
and our clear choice is bricks and mortar.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:26.460
- [Narrator] This comes
at an uncertain time

00:00:26.460 --> 00:00:28.590
for struggling clothing retailers.

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:31.380
Retailers like Forever 21, JC Penney,

00:00:31.380 --> 00:00:33.540
and Neiman Marcus have closed stores

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:35.250
and filed for bankruptcy.

00:00:35.250 --> 00:00:38.940
And while a UBS report shows
up to 80,000 retail stores

00:00:38.940 --> 00:00:41.040
will close in the next five years,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:45.030
Primark plans to expand to 60
stores in the same timeframe.

00:00:45.030 --> 00:00:46.410
- [Speaker] Is driving
two hours from Toronto

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.210
to Buffalo to shop at Primark worth it?

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:50.625
Heck yes, I used to
fly six hours to do so.

00:00:50.625 --> 00:00:54.420
- So how is Primark expanding
without online shopping?

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.970
This is the economics of Primark.

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:00.390
- Fashion retail is
incredibly competitive,

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:01.440
more so than ever.

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.620
There's not many players,
especially in fast fashion

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:06.690
that are making fat margins.

00:01:06.690 --> 00:01:09.152
Pretty much the thinking everywhere is

00:01:09.152 --> 00:01:11.520
that you have to have
this online presence.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:12.870
- [Narrator] But online shopping is

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:15.587
actually incredibly
expensive for retailers.

00:01:15.587 --> 00:01:17.460
- You have to have a logistical hub

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.650
from which you would dispatch the goods

00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:21.990
and then you'd probably have
a local distribution center

00:01:21.990 --> 00:01:24.000
from which you have to
do last mile delivery.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.310
Each step of that just is
an extra layer of cost,

00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:27.838
it just eats away at the margin at which

00:01:27.838 --> 00:01:31.836
in fashion is very, very slim
anyway, but the real killer

00:01:31.836 --> 00:01:35.032
for e-commerce is somebody
who buys something

00:01:35.032 --> 00:01:38.366
on the internet is far more
likely to return that product

00:01:38.366 --> 00:01:40.591
than somebody who goes into a store.

00:01:40.591 --> 00:01:41.670
- [Narrator] Returns are one

00:01:41.670 --> 00:01:44.322
of the biggest profit
losses for online retailers.

00:01:44.322 --> 00:01:47.910
On average, processing
a return for $100 worth

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:51.600
of merchandise bought online
costs a company 26.50.

00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.200
For years, online retailers
just ate these expenses.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:56.033
Only now are more

00:01:56.033 --> 00:01:59.730
of them deciding to pass the
cost back to the consumer.

00:01:59.730 --> 00:02:01.650
- Primark have just stayed
right from the beginning,

00:02:01.650 --> 00:02:04.500
we don't think we can make
money out of e-commerce.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:05.430
We're not even gonna try.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:06.676
We're gonna focus all our resources

00:02:06.676 --> 00:02:09.485
on the physical store
and protect our margins.

00:02:09.485 --> 00:02:11.070
- [Narrator] And that wasn't an issue

00:02:11.070 --> 00:02:12.960
for the retailer until.

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:16.137
- Now Primark is expected to close all 189

00:02:16.137 --> 00:02:18.630
of its UK stores tonight

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:21.570
and it doesn't have an online
offering to fall back on.

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:24.540
- E-commerce was a lifeline
for a lot of retailers

00:02:24.540 --> 00:02:25.590
during the pandemic.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.435
They couldn't open their
stores, but they were still able

00:02:27.435 --> 00:02:30.090
to make some sales on the internet.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:31.710
- [Narrator] But Primark didn't budge,

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:32.543
even as consumers urged

00:02:32.543 --> 00:02:35.060
for it to offer online shopping and even

00:02:35.060 --> 00:02:38.970
after its revenue dropped
by almost 2 billion pounds.

00:02:38.970 --> 00:02:40.950
- And I think their
confidence in this model

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:44.160
of having no digital sales
and only using stores I think,

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.704
was probably shaken by the pandemic.

00:02:46.704 --> 00:02:48.270
Who knows what would've happened

00:02:48.270 --> 00:02:49.530
if it had gone on for longer?

00:02:49.530 --> 00:02:51.840
I think the closures
weren't quite long enough

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:52.773
to really tip the scales

00:02:52.773 --> 00:02:56.100
and force them to fundamentally
rethink the model.

00:02:56.100 --> 00:02:58.080
- [Narrator] Instead of
traditional online shopping,

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.140
in 2022, Primark launched
a trial service called,

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:04.170
Click and Collect in select UK stores.

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.570
Customers can select
certain products online,

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:08.247
then pick up their orders in store.

00:03:08.247 --> 00:03:11.610
So Primark still has some
cost to maintain its website

00:03:11.610 --> 00:03:14.232
but it's cheaper than
running a true online shop.

00:03:14.232 --> 00:03:17.490
- So Primark makes a sale,
but they don't have any

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.680
of the logistical costs
that would be involved

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.141
in packing it up and then
shipping it out to your home.

00:03:23.141 --> 00:03:25.620
- [Narrator] And there's
another bonus for Primark.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:26.916
Since the customer has to go inside

00:03:26.916 --> 00:03:29.860
to collect their purchase,
they're likely to browse around

00:03:29.860 --> 00:03:32.057
and maybe even buy more.

00:03:32.057 --> 00:03:33.750
- They make the sale online,

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:35.026
but then they have a good chance

00:03:35.026 --> 00:03:38.370
of making some extra sales
when that person visits them.

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:39.690
- [Narrator] Consumer psychologists

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.460
call this shopping momentum.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:44.490
Essentially, once you've already
decided to make a purchase,

00:03:44.490 --> 00:03:47.280
it's easier to justify
adding more to your cart.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:49.650
- It always feels just overwhelmingly busy

00:03:49.650 --> 00:03:52.440
with people who are in
there looking for bargains.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:53.940
- [Narrator] And it's not just clothing.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.310
Primark lures consumers with beauty, home,

00:03:56.310 --> 00:03:59.280
and travel products similar
to a department store.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:01.223
- What we like to say about
Primark here in the US is

00:04:01.223 --> 00:04:04.665
that we're all treasure without the hunt.

00:04:04.665 --> 00:04:06.300
- [Narrator] So customers may go in

00:04:06.300 --> 00:04:09.024
for a new t-shirt and
leave with a suitcase.

00:04:09.024 --> 00:04:11.190
- [Speaker] Primark that I
can drive to and don't have

00:04:11.190 --> 00:04:13.503
to fly to is a dream because
that means there's no limit

00:04:13.503 --> 00:04:15.240
to what I can buy and bring home.

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.430
- [Narrator] To carry this
vast selection of items,

00:04:17.430 --> 00:04:19.391
Primark finds large retail spaces

00:04:19.391 --> 00:04:22.002
because it relies on selling a lot.

00:04:22.002 --> 00:04:24.330
It's one of the largest
retailers in the UK

00:04:24.330 --> 00:04:27.060
by volume and one of the
largest across all of Europe.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.170
Last year, Primark made about
9.5 billion in sales revenue

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:34.800
in part by selling a lot of
things at low profit margins.

00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:36.273
And it does this by sourcing a lot

00:04:36.273 --> 00:04:38.177
of its clothing from Asia.

00:04:38.177 --> 00:04:41.100
Combined with not
offering online shopping,

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.320
this enables Primark
to sell its merchandise

00:04:43.320 --> 00:04:44.543
at lower prices,

00:04:44.543 --> 00:04:48.030
even lower than many other
fast fashion retailers.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.520
- Where they do have
serious rivals on price is

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.659
with the digital only players
that like Shein for example,

00:04:53.659 --> 00:04:56.550
they are often even cheaper than Primark.

00:04:56.550 --> 00:04:57.383
- [Narrator] But according

00:04:57.383 --> 00:04:58.980
to The National Retail Federation,

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:01.349
80% of shopping still happens in store,

00:05:01.349 --> 00:05:02.609
giving Primark an edge

00:05:02.609 --> 00:05:05.809
over its digital only
competitors, even so,

00:05:05.809 --> 00:05:07.860
its in-store shopping model got off

00:05:07.860 --> 00:05:09.900
to a rocky start in the US.

00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:14.004
Primark opened its first US
store in Boston back in 2015.

00:05:14.004 --> 00:05:16.620
Although it was already popular in the UK,

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:19.020
its US expansion started slowly.

00:05:19.020 --> 00:05:21.364
Over four years, it opened nine stores.

00:05:21.364 --> 00:05:23.189
After years of experimentation,

00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:25.061
Primark learned it would have to change

00:05:25.061 --> 00:05:26.880
to succeed in the US.

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:27.990
- When we first opened,

00:05:27.990 --> 00:05:30.870
we took a similar store size
that we would've had in Europe.

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:32.310
- [Narrator] But when
Primark opened its stores

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:33.720
across the northeast,

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:35.850
it struggled to find
the right store density.

00:05:35.850 --> 00:05:38.038
- What they found in
the US is that retail is

00:05:38.038 --> 00:05:39.990
so much more competitive.

00:05:39.990 --> 00:05:44.579
There's a lot more retail
in downtown areas in the US

00:05:44.579 --> 00:05:46.860
than there is in the UK, for example.

00:05:46.860 --> 00:05:49.140
So you just can't support a massive store.

00:05:49.140 --> 00:05:52.122
It's just not possible to get
enough revenue for the size.

00:05:52.122 --> 00:05:54.390
- To support its large store sizes,

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:56.390
it needed a lot of foot traffic.

00:05:56.390 --> 00:05:58.551
But unlike the UK stores
which were located

00:05:58.551 --> 00:06:02.855
in constantly busy areas, US
stores were mostly in malls

00:06:02.855 --> 00:06:06.060
where during the week,
foot traffic dwindled.

00:06:06.060 --> 00:06:09.060
- We learned that actually,
35,000 square foot

00:06:09.060 --> 00:06:12.480
of retail selling space is
actually the sweet spot for us.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:14.430
- They didn't face the commercial pressure

00:06:14.430 --> 00:06:17.280
that a publicly owned
company might have faced

00:06:17.280 --> 00:06:19.127
to suddenly open 100 stores

00:06:19.127 --> 00:06:21.060
and get really big really quickly

00:06:21.060 --> 00:06:23.430
and then possibly risk disaster

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:24.840
because they hadn't thought it through.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:26.730
- The culture of Primark has always been

00:06:26.730 --> 00:06:29.010
to take our time and to test and learn.

00:06:29.010 --> 00:06:30.294
And we've done that in every country

00:06:30.294 --> 00:06:32.790
where we've introduced the brand.

00:06:32.790 --> 00:06:36.270
This wasn't about growing
market share at all costs.

00:06:36.270 --> 00:06:38.421
- [Narrator] And the overall
retail market is shifting.

00:06:38.421 --> 00:06:41.340
Online shopping hit a peak
during Covid, but now,

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:44.397
growth is beginning to slow
as people return to stores.

00:06:44.397 --> 00:06:46.586
- And I think that's
never been so important

00:06:46.586 --> 00:06:48.871
as it is since we came outta Covid.

00:06:48.871 --> 00:06:51.657
I think there has been
a massive rediscovery

00:06:51.657 --> 00:06:55.620
of being in malls, being
together, physical shopping,

00:06:55.620 --> 00:06:56.610
and I think as a business

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:59.190
that took the decision a long time ago

00:06:59.190 --> 00:07:01.998
to have that as our core strategy,

00:07:01.998 --> 00:07:06.039
has continued to allow
us to be successful.

00:07:06.039 --> 00:07:08.706
(upbeat music)

